📦 Sliding Window Protocol with Piggybacking
🎯 Objective
To implement a simulation demonstrating the Sliding Window mechanism for flow control and the Piggybacking technique to reduce protocol overhead.

📘 Core Concepts
📐 Sliding Window
Allows the sender to have multiple "in-flight" packets (up to the window size 
W
W) before requiring an ACK. This fills the pipe (bandwidth-delay product) more effectively than Stop-and-Wait.

🎒 Piggybacking
Instead of sending a dedicated ACK packet, the receiver attaches the acknowledgment sequence number to its own outgoing data frame. This saves bandwidth and reduces the number of small packets in the network.

🖧 Simulation Environment
Topology: Point-to-point connection between:

PC0 (192.168.1.2)

PC1 (192.168.1.3)

Tool: Cisco Packet Tracer — Simulation Mode

🔍 Observation Instructions
Open sliding_window.pkt.

Observe the PDU Details.

Look for the ACK field within a Data PDU coming back from the receiver.

Note that the sender continues to transmit while the ACK is still in transit, provided the window limit isn't reached.
